{
  "requirement": "TBV selects a compiled-in outline language from the file extension and uses\nthe plain-text outline for other files. Text search uses regular expressions.\nStructural search uses the requested language's syntax capability under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.language_provider.structural_search`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.sources.provider_defaults`. I did not request implementation source. The assessment rests on the supplied test text.\n\n**Obligations derived from the requirement:**\n1. A compiled-in outline language is selected from the file extension.\n2. Files with other extensions get the plain-text outline.\n3. Text search uses regular expressions.\n4. Structural search uses the requested language's syntax capability. Behavior under `requirements.sdk.outlines.language_provider.structural_search` is cited context and is not reassessed.\n\n**Mapping to tests:**\n- **Extension selects a language.**\n  - `tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` parses `visible.rs` through `Sources` and asserts the fingerprint scheme is `rust-structural-v1`.\n  - `src/sources/tests.rs#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` parses `file.rs` with no explicit language and asserts that `fn[provided]` resolves in the outline. The same test checks that an explicit language wins over the `.py` extension.\n- **Other files get the plain-text outline.** The same `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` test parses `note.unknown`. It asserts the scheme `plain-text-structural-v1` and an outline of 3 items (the root plus two nonblank lines).\n- **Text search uses regular expressions.** `tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository]` searches with `^n[e]{2}dle$` and asserts 3 matches. A literal-string search could not produce that result.\n- **Structural search uses the requested language.**\n  - `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` first searches with a rust query over a tree containing both `.rs` and `.py` files. It then searches with a python query and asserts exactly one match, in `script.py`. This shows the requested language selects the syntax capability and the files searched.\n  - It also checks limit and completeness handling and scope handling.\n  - The `list_read_only_provider_…` test covers a malformed rule (error), an unknown language `not-a-language` (error), and a language that matches nothing in the parsed source (complete, empty result).\n  - `src/sources/tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` checks that the parsed snapshot and scope are used.\n\n**Gaps:** I found none. The unsupported-language default belongs to the cited `language_provider.structural_search` requirement and is not reassessed here. Every clause of the subject has a test with concrete assertions, and the tests carry `{test}` references to the subject.",
  "references": "bdf30d9640f8129207adddb6d47d3d38ba6881940cf4a8e3f719985fbeeb7a80",
